From 3c72916dd938cc0e2383781ae3e0ac5f1d29b7c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MiaAI-Lab <MiaAI-Lab@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 04:56:16 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] glm_moe_dsa: the index scores' query rope writes its second
 half to element 32 + i

tf_glm_dsa_scores ropes each head's 64-wide query tail in place in
shared memory, pair (2 i, 2 i + 1) moving to (i, 32 + i), the layout the
index keys are written in. The shared rows are XOR-swizzled in 8-element
chunks, and at(row, u) takes a chunk, but the second half went to
at(hh, D / 4 + i / 8): chunk 32 + i / 8, element 256 + ..., two heads
down. It overwrote another head's roped pairs, racing that head's own
writes, and left dims 32..63 of every head unroped (heads 30 and 31 wrote
into the first key buffers before they were staged).

The index scores, the picks and every sparse prompt row after them were
wrong and changed from run to run: two runs of one 6.6k-token text gave
mean KL 0.03 and 3% top-1 flips past position 2048, the dense limit,
with the kernel's inputs byte-identical and its scores differing in
nearly every visible entry (correlation 0.98). The second half now goes
to at(hh, (32 + i) / 8) + i % 8.

The bug is in the base engine (commit 168e0e2); the read-before-write
reorder in the previous commit was needed but not sufficient.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UQVvNktFCvQrvpNUbqtqBX
---
 zig/kernels/cuda/glm_glue_attn.cuh | 6 ++++--
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/zig/kernels/cuda/glm_glue_attn.cuh b/zig/kernels/cuda/glm_glue_attn.cuh
index cfd9f09..d835655 100644
--- a/zig/kernels/cuda/glm_glue_attn.cuh
+++ b/zig/kernels/cuda/glm_glue_attn.cuh
@@ -911,7 +911,9 @@ extern "C" __global__ void __launch_bounds__(128)
   // The queries' rope, in place while q's rows are the only thing in shared: then the fragments read roped queries.
   // Pair i of (2 i, 2 i + 1) moves to (i, 32 + i), so a lane writes elements another lane has yet to read: every pair
   // is read and roped into registers first, then (after a barrier) written. Done in one pass, the reads and writes of
-  // a head's lanes raced: the index scores, the picks and every sparse row after them changed from run to run.
+  // a head's lanes raced. The second half once went to chunk 32 + i / 8 (element 256 + ..., two heads down) instead of
+  // element 32 + i: it overwrote another head's roped pairs, racing that head's own writes, and left this head's
+  // dims 32..63 unroped. The index scores, the picks and every sparse row after them changed from run to run.
   const int p0q = pos[0] + (int)r;
   constexpr int PER = HP * (ROPE / 2) / 128;  // pairs a thread ropes (8)
   float2 rot[PER];
@@ -929,7 +931,7 @@ extern "C" __global__ void __launch_bounds__(128)
     const int idx = threadIdx.x + 128 * k, hh = idx / (ROPE / 2), i = idx % (ROPE / 2);
     if (hh >= heads) continue;
     sm[at(hh, i / 8) + i % 8] = __float2bfloat16_rn(rot[k].x);
-    sm[at(hh, D / 4 + i / 8) + i % 8] = __float2bfloat16_rn(rot[k].y);
+    sm[at(hh, (ROPE / 2 + i) / 8) + i % 8] = __float2bfloat16_rn(rot[k].y);  // element 32 + i: at() takes 8-wide chunks
   }
   __syncthreads();
   uint32_t aq[D / 16][2][4];
